
*********************Installation of Docker on Linux*****************
->go to https://docs.docker.com/engine/install
->copy all commands together from
  "1.set up dockers apt repository" and run in terminal together
->copy the commands from
  "2.install the docker packages" and run
->sudo grupadd docker
->sudo usermod -aG docker $(whoami)
->sudo reboot



*******************Basic & Inspection Commands**************
1.Check Docker version:
->docker --version

2.Display system-wide information:
->docker info

3.Image Management
Download an image from Docker Hub:
->docker pull ubuntu:latest

4.List local images:
->Docker images

5.Remove a local image
->docker rmi <image_id_or_name>

6.Container Lifecycle
Run a container interactively:
->docker run -it ubuntu:latest bash

7.Run a container in the background (detached mode) with port mapping:
->docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-web-server nginx:latest

8.List running containers:
->docker ps

9.List all containers (running and stopped):
->docker ps -a

10.stop a running container
->docker stop <container_id_or_name>

11.Start a stopped container:
->docker start <container_id_or_name>


12.Remove a stopped container:
->docker rm <container_id_or_name>

13.Troubleshooting & Logs
View container logs:
->docker logs <container_id_or_name>

14.Execute a command inside a running container:
->docker exec -it <container_id_or_name> bash

15.restart the contantainer
->docker restart my-app-container

16.Check Live Resource Usage of active container
->docker stats

17.Deletes all currently stopped containers on your system
->docker container prune

18.Inspecting container details:
->docker inspect <container_id_or_name>

19.list all volumes
->docker volume ls

20.docker volume inspect 
->docker volume inspect <volume_name>

21.remove unused volumes
->docker volume rm <volume_name>

22.Show container port mappings: Quick check to see what ports are exposed.bash
->docker port <container_name>


***************Creation of Docker File********************
# 1. Choose the base operating system or runtime
FROM node:20-alpine

# 2. Set the working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /app

# 3. Copy files from your computer into the container
COPY package*.json ./

# 4. Run setup/installation commands inside the container
RUN npm install

# 5. Copy the rest of your project files
COPY . .

# 6. Document the network port the app listens on
EXPOSE 3000

# 7. Define the default command to start your application
CMD ["npm", "start"]


********************Image building***************
docker build -t myapp:latest .

***************Make an Container of an image and run it in diff ways*************
1.docker run -d -p 80:80 --name react-container my-react-app:latest
2.docker run -it myapp:latest /bin/sh
3.docker run --rm myapp:latest
4.docker run -e NODE_ENV=production -e DB_PORT=5432 -d myapp:latest


*****Pushing to Docker hub*******
1.docker login
2.docker tag my-app:latest username/my-app:v1
3.docker push username/my-app:v1


*********pulling frm Docker hub**********
1.docker pull username/my-app:v1
2.docker run my-app  // run will automatically pull the image 




***************Docker Volumes & Bind Mounts*******************
----Docker Volumes--------
1.docker volume create my-vol
2.docker run -d -v my-vol:/app/data my-image


-----------Bind Mounts----------
1.docker run -d -v $(pwd):/app my-image







*************Docker Compose***********
1.Start services in detached mode:
->docker compose up -d

2.View running compose services:
->docker compose ps

3.View compose logs:
->docker compose logs -f

4.Stop and remove containers, networks, and volumes:
->docker compose down -v


***********Network Commands************
1.Create a network:
->docker network create --driver bridge <network_name>

2.list networks:
->docker network ls

3.Connect a running container to a network:bash
->docker network connect <network_name> <container_name>

4.Disconnect a container from a network:bash
->docker network disconnect <network_name> <container_name>

5.Inspect a network (See which containers are attached to it):bash
->docker network inspect <network_name>



































